CH = dict(num=6, title="The Beginnings of Indian Civilisation", tag="History", theme="Indus-Sarasvatī (Harappan) civilisation",
 intro="After early farming villages came civilisation. This chapter defines 'civilisation' and studies the earliest one in the Indian Subcontinent — the Harappan, Indus or Indus-Sarasvatī civilisation — its planned cities, water management, farming, trade, crafts, art and eventual decline.",
 big_questions=["What is a civilisation?", "What was the earliest civilisation of the Indian Subcontinent?", "What were its major achievements?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "6.1 What Is a Civilisation?"),
 ("p", "'Civilisation' means an advanced stage of human society. Here it must have at least these characteristics:"),
 ("table", ["Characteristic", "Why it matters"], [
  ["Government and administration", "To manage a complex society and its many activities"],
  ["Urbanism", "Town-planning, growth of cities and their management, water management and drainage"],
  ["Variety of crafts", "Managing raw materials (stone, metal) and producing finished goods (ornaments, tools)"],
  ["Trade — internal and external", "To exchange goods within a city or region and with distant regions"],
  ["Some form of writing", "To keep records and communicate"],
  ["Cultural ideas", "About life and the world, expressed through art, architecture, literature, oral traditions, customs"],
  ["Productive agriculture", "Enough to feed not just villages but cities"]]),
 ("ul", ["Civilisation began at different times in different places: **Mesopotamia** (modern Iraq and Syria) about **6,000 years ago**; **Egypt** a few centuries later; the Indian story begins in the **north-west** of the Subcontinent.",
  "Timeline: Mesopotamian beginning ~4000 BCE; Egyptian a bit later; **Indus-Sarasvatī civilisation about 2600 to 1900 BCE** (mature phase)."]),
 ("h2", "6.2 From Village to City"),
 ("ul", ["The plains of **Punjab** and **Sindh** are watered by the **Indus and its tributaries** — fertile and good for farming. A **tributary** is a river flowing into a larger river.",
  "A few millenniums ago the **Sarasvatī River** flowed from the Himalayan foothills through Haryana, Punjab, parts of Rajasthan and Gujarat.",
  "From about **3500 BCE** villages grew into towns; around **2600 BCE** towns became **cities** — called the **First Urbanisation of India**.",
  "**Names:** Indus, Harappan, Indus-Sarasvatī or Sindhu-Sarasvatī civilisation; people are **Harappans**. It is one of the world's oldest civilisations.",
  "**Why 'Harappan'?** Harappa (now in Pakistan's Punjab) was the first city excavated, in **1920–21**; Harappa and Mohenjo-daro were identified by 1924.",
  "**Why not 'Indus Valley'?** The term is obsolete — the civilisation extended well beyond the Indus region."]),
 ("table", ["City", "Modern location"], [
  ["Harappa", "Punjab (Pakistan)"],
  ["Mohenjo-daro", "Sindh (Pakistan)"],
  ["Ganweriwala", "Cholistan desert, Pakistan"],
  ["Dholavira", "Rann of Kutch, Gujarat"],
  ["Lothal", "Gujarat (dockyard)"],
  ["Rakhigarhi", "Haryana"],
  ["Kalibangan", "Rajasthan"],
  ["Farmana, Bhirrana, Banawali", "Haryana (smaller sites / towns)"]]),
 ("h3", "The Sarasvatī River"),
 ("ul", ["Today called **Ghaggar** in India and **Hakra** in Pakistan (Ghaggar-Hakra); now **seasonal**, flowing only in the rains.",
  "Mentioned in the **Ṛig Veda** as goddess and river flowing 'from the mountain to the sea'; later texts say it dried up.",
  "Its basin has a high density of sites — two major cities (Rakhigarhi, Ganweriwala), smaller ones and towns."]),
 ("h2", "6.3 Town-Planning and Water Management"),
 ("ul", ["Larger cities were built to **precise plans**: wide streets, often aligned to cardinal directions; **fortifications**; two parts — **upper town** (elite: rulers, officials, administrators, priests) and **lower town** (common people). **Dholavira had three zones**, and used stone foundations.",
  "Large buildings such as **warehouses** for collective use; houses of all sizes along streets and lanes with the **same quality of construction**; buildings generally of **bricks**.",
  "**Great Bath, Mohenjo-daro:** ~12 × 7 m tank, waterproofed with **natural bitumen** over carefully laid bricks; small rooms around, a well, a drain to empty it. Purpose debated: public bath (ruled out because most houses had bathrooms), bath for royalty, or ritual use.",
  "**Drainage:** bathing areas in homes connected to covered drains below streets (e.g. Lothal). **Wells:** hundreds in Mohenjo-daro (about 700 counted); other places used ponds, streams, reservoirs.",
  "**Dholavira:** at least six large reservoirs cut in rock or built of stone, linked by underground drains for water harvesting; largest reservoir about 73 m long. This implies organised labour, a ruler and municipal authority."]),
 ("h2", "6.4 Food, Farming and Trade"),
 ("table", ["Area", "Details"], [
  ["Crops", "Barley, wheat, some millets, sometimes rice; pulses and vegetables. **First in Eurasia to grow cotton**"],
  ["Tools", "Farm tools including the **plough** (clay model from Banawali)"],
  ["Animals / fish", "Domesticated animals for meat; fishing in rivers and sea (bones found)"],
  ["Diet evidence", "Dairy products; also turmeric, ginger, banana traces in cooking pots"],
  ["Exports", "Ornaments, timber, objects of daily use, probably gold, cotton and some food"],
  ["Imports", "Probably copper (scarce at home)"],
  ["Ornaments", "Beads of **carnelian** (reddish semi-precious stone from Gujarat) with special drilling techniques; **conch-shell bangles**"],
  ["Metals", "Copper; **bronze** (copper + tin) for tools, pots, pans, figurines"],
  ["Routes", "Land, rivers and sea — the first intensive **maritime activity** in India; coastal settlements in Gujarat and Sindh"],
  ["Lothal dockyard", "Basin 217 m × 36 m (a little over two football grounds long)"],
  ["Seals", "Thousands of small **steatite** seals with animals (unicorn, bull, horned tiger) and writing signs; used to identify goods and traders. **The script remains undeciphered**"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "Trade evidence abroad: Harappan carnelian beads at **Susa (Iran)** and an ivory comb on the coast of **Oman**."),
 ("h2", "6.5 The Lives of the Ancients"),
 ("ul", ["**Daily objects:** bronze mirror, terracotta pots, stone weights, bronze chisel, games board, terracotta whistle (toys and games for adults and children).",
  "**Cultural objects:** bronze **'Dancing Girl'** (10.8 cm, bangles covering an arm); so-called 'Priest King' statuette; terracotta figure seated in a *namaste*; **swastika** seal; seal of a three-faced deity on a platform with animals; a Lothal pot design that seems to show the story of the **thirsty crow**."]),
 ("h2", "6.6 The End or a New Beginning?"),
 ("ul", ["Around **1900 BCE** the cities were abandoned one by one; people dispersed to hundreds or thousands of small rural settlements; the old administration disappeared.",
  "Earlier theory of invasion or warfare is not supported — **no trace of warfare**, no army or weapons found; a relatively **peaceful** civilisation.",
  "**Agreed causes:** (1) a worldwide **climate change from 2200 BCE** — less rain, drier phase, harder farming; (2) the **Sarasvatī dried up** in its central basin, so Kalibangan and Banawali were abandoned.",
  "Cities disappeared but much of the culture and technology **survived** into the next phase of Indian civilisation.",
  "B.B. Lal: Harappan society was one of 'mutual accommodation', not exploitation, with no glaring rich–poor gap."]),
 ("h2", "6.7 Quick Reference"),
 ("qa", [
  ("What is a civilisation? List four characteristics.", "An advanced stage of society; government, urbanism, crafts, trade, writing, cultural ideas, productive agriculture."),
  ("Why is it called Harappan civilisation?", "Harappa was the first city excavated (1920–21)."),
  ("Why is 'Indus Valley' no longer used?", "Sites lie far beyond the Indus region, including the Sarasvatī basin, Gujarat and Haryana."),
  ("When did the Indus-Sarasvatī civilisation flourish?", "About 2600 to 1900 BCE, with village growth from 3500 BCE."),
  ("Describe Harappan town-planning.", "Planned, fortified cities with wide streets, upper and lower towns, brick buildings, warehouses and drains."),
  ("What was the Great Bath?", "A waterproofed tank (~12 × 7 m) at Mohenjo-daro, probably used for ritual; its exact purpose is debated."),
  ("How did Harappans manage water?", "Wells, drains, reservoirs; Dholavira's rock-cut reservoirs and underground drains."),
  ("What did the Harappans grow and eat?", "Wheat, barley, millets, some rice, pulses, vegetables, cotton; dairy, meat and fish; also turmeric, ginger and banana."),
  ("Why was Lothal important?", "It had a huge dockyard (217 × 36 m) for sea trade."),
  ("What were seals used for?", "Identifying goods and traders; they bear animal figures and undeciphered writing."),
  ("What caused the decline?", "Climate change (drier from 2200 BCE) and the drying of the Sarasvatī River."),
  ("Why do we think it was peaceful?", "No evidence of warfare, armies or weapons.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["A civilisation needs **government, cities, crafts, trade, writing, culture and surplus farming**.",
  "The **Indus-Sarasvatī (Harappan) civilisation (~2600–1900 BCE)** was India's first urbanisation, spread across present India and Pakistan.",
  "Planned cities, **water management**, cotton, bronze, bead-making, seals and **maritime trade** were its signature achievements.",
  "Its script is **undeciphered**; it appears peaceful and egalitarian.",
  "**Climate change and the drying of the Sarasvatī** led to its decline, but its culture lived on."])
